  int x = 5, y = 0;

  if (y == 0) {
    printf("y is 0\n");
  } else if (y == 1) {
    printf("y is 1\n");
  } else {
    printf("y is 2\n");
  }

  return 0;
}

This code first declares two integer variables, and sets them to 5 and 0 respectively. It then checks three different cases for the value of the boolean variable y. If y is 0, it prints "y is 0". If y is 1, it prints "y is 1". Otherwise, it prints "y is 2". Finally, it returns 0 to indicate that the program has finished executing successfully.
